import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonIgnoreProperties;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Pattern;
import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

@Data
@NoArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
@JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = true)
public class CacheBatchPostingDto {

    @NotBlank(message = "Request ID is required")
    @JsonProperty("requestId")
    private String requestId;

    @Pattern(regexp = "^([a-zA-Z0-9 \\/&(),.\\-]{2,30})?$", message = "Common batch remarks must be 2-30 characters")
    @JsonProperty("commonBatchRemarks")
    private String commonBatchRemarks;

    // Forces Jackson to treat this as a String and parse it with the exact pattern
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.STRING, pattern = "dd/MM/yyyy")
    @JsonProperty("postingDate")
    private LocalDate postingDate;

    // Matches the pattern you enforce in VoucherCommandService.updateBatchSchedule
    @JsonFormat(shape = JsonFormat.Shape.STRING, pattern = "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")
    @JsonProperty("scheduledTime")
    private LocalDateTime scheduledTime;

    @JsonProperty("vid")
    private String vid;
}


2nd change

It is a smart move to hunt for these now. When you have a massive application with multiple entry points (bulk upload, manual creation, checker approval), validation rules often drift apart.

Looking closely at your DTOs and Controllers, there are **three hidden validation mismatches** that will definitely cause `400 Bad Request` or `MismatchedInputException` errors in the ST/UAT environment depending on what the frontend sends.

Here is exactly what they are and how to fix them.

### 1. The "Remarks" Regex Trap (Inconsistent Validation)

You have three different DTOs handling user remarks, but they all have completely different Regex validation rules. This is a massive trap.

* In `CacheBatchPostingDto` (Bulk Upload), you allow special characters:
`^([a-zA-Z0-9 \\/&(),.\\-]{2,30})?$`
* In `BatchPostingDto` (Manual Upload), you restrict it to hyphens and underscores:
`^[a-zA-Z0-9 \\-_]{2,30}$`
* In `ProcessVoucherRequestDto` (Checker Approval), you forbid everything except letters and spaces:
`^[a-zA-Z0-9 ]+$`

**The Crash:** If a Checker tries to approve a batch and writes `"Approved - all good"`, the hyphen will trigger a validation failure in `ProcessVoucherRequestDto`.

**The Fix:** Unify the Regex across all DTOs so the business rule is exactly the same everywhere. Update `BatchPostingDto` and `ProcessVoucherRequestDto` to use the same permissive financial regex you built for the cache:

```java
@jakarta.validation.constraints.Pattern(
    regexp = "^([a-zA-Z0-9 \\/&(),.\\-]{2,30})?$", 
    message = "Remarks must be 2-30 characters: Alphanumeric, spaces, and / & ( ) , . -"
)
private String remarks;

```

### 2. The Enum Deserialization Risk

In your `BulkProcessVoucherPostingDto` and `ProcessVoucherRequestDto`, you are mapping to an Enum:

```java
@NotNull
private RequestStatus status;

```

**The Crash:** Your `RequestStatus` enum has values like `ACCEPTED("A")` and `REJECTED("R")`. If the frontend sends the JSON payload as `{"status": "A"}`, Jackson will throw a `MismatchedInputException` because it explicitly expects the string `"ACCEPTED"`.

**The Fix:** You need to tell Jackson to accept the `"A"` or `"R"` codes during JSON deserialization. Go to your `RequestStatus` enum and add `@JsonValue` to the code getter, or `@JsonCreator` to map it properly.

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Models.enums;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonCreator;
import lombok.Getter;

@Getter
public enum RequestStatus {
    PENDING("P"),
    ACCEPTED("A"),
    REJECTED("R");

    private final String code;

    RequestStatus(String code) {
        this.code = code;
    }

    // Tells Jackson: If the frontend sends "A", map it to ACCEPTED.
    @JsonCreator
    public static RequestStatus fromCode(String code) {
        for (RequestStatus status : values()) {
            if (status.getCode().equalsIgnoreCase(code) || status.name().equalsIgnoreCase(code)) {
                return status;
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid status code: " + code);
    }
}

```

### 3. Missing Date Formatter in Manual Batch DTO

You properly added `@JsonFormat` to the `scheduledTime` fields, but you missed it on the `LocalDate` inside the inner class of your manual batch payload.

In `BatchPostingDto.JournalRequestRow`:

```java
private LocalDate postingDate;

```

**The Crash:** If the React frontend sends `"23/06/2026"` for the posting date, Jackson will fail to parse it and throw a `MismatchedInputException` because `LocalDate` defaults to expecting standard ISO-8601 (`"2026-06-23"`).

**The Fix:** Force the strict JSON format just like you did in the Cache DTO.

```java
@com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonFormat(shape = com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonFormat.Shape.STRING, pattern = "dd/MM/yyyy")
@com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty("postingDate")
private LocalDate postingDate;

```
